    ('7ee6d3f9-e9cc-43d1-8747-17d15e31ba16'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An ancient city built on the Hippodamian grid plan around 180 BC by a Greco-Bactrian king. Greek urban planning transplanted to the edge of South Asia.', 'Opposite Taxila, the Sirkap Remains trace a grid city built by King Demetrius after he invaded around 180 BC: one main avenue, fifteen perpendicular streets, a wall 4.8 kilometers long.', 'teaser-card-s024:7ee6d3f9-e9cc-43d1-8747-17d15e31ba16:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '63baadef32515bef2ae285e9a7dc6cbf1f9cd94d74db61834b17ac22db399840|f433302eb29f0500d28a4b7139b17eb01972fae5508fbf7d24cad7a9d5f59933|f433302eb29f0500d28a4b7139b17eb01972fae5508fbf7d24cad7a9d5f59933', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Opposite Taxila, the Sirkap Remains trace a grid city built by King Demetrius after he invaded around 180 BC: one main avenue, fifteen perpendicular streets, a wall 4.8 kilometers long."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Sirkap Remains lie opposite Taxila [S1]; Sirkap is a city laid out on a grid plan [S5]; it was built by King Demetrius [S2]; he built it after he invaded around 180 BC [S2]; it has one main avenue [S6]; it has fifteen perpendicular streets [S6]; its wall is 4.8 kilometers long [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-044 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sirkap Remains is an archaeological site (ancient city) opposite Taxila (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirkap]; The city followed a grid plan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirkap]; It was built by King Demetrius [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirkap]; Demetrius invaded around 180 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirkap]; One main avenue [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirkap]; Fifteen perpendicular streets [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirkap]; Surrounding wall 4.8 kilometers long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirkap]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7ef5997f-b5ec-4559-9611-8016a7191f0a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The remains of the Late Roman hillfort were discovered around 1900 and excavated for the first time between 1907 and 1932.', 'Built around 300 AD after the Roman limes was overrun, Katzenberg Hillfort near Mayen was a refuge: in time of danger, the people of the Roman vicus could escape to it.', 'teaser-card-s024:7ef5997f-b5ec-4559-9611-8016a7191f0a:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 168 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '879997c80533a1449ac8ed2d8865fc72ffaad77408ad973c43f3c2a6f693a8a6|b81eaa8ebad0bcb31f9aa87342bfe7f058c1e7b8492f7cac3287acc06d4b93d8|b81eaa8ebad0bcb31f9aa87342bfe7f058c1e7b8492f7cac3287acc06d4b93d8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Built around 300 AD after the Roman limes was overrun, Katzenberg Hillfort near Mayen was a refuge: in time of danger, the people of the Roman vicus could escape to it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Katzenberg Hillfort was built around 300 AD [S2]; it was built after the Roman limes had been overrun [S2]; it is near Mayen [S1]; it was a refuge fort [S1]; in time of danger the inhabitants of the Roman vicus could escape to it [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-044 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Katzenberg Hillfort near Mayen is a Roman refuge fort (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katzenberg_Hillfort]; Built around 300 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katzenberg_Hillfort]; Built after the Roman limes was overrun [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katzenberg_Hillfort]; People of the Roman vicus could escape to it in time of danger [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katzenberg_Hillfort]; It served as a refuge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katzenberg_Hillfort]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7f4207f8-30df-4aa9-850e-6873fcab2bb3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Neolithic chambered cairn 60 metres long and 25 metres wide, of the Severn-Cotswold type. These trapezoid mounds with internal stone chambers were built for collective burial.', 'Inside Fairy Toot, a Somerset barrow opened between 1787 and 1835, lay two rows of cells of immense stones set edgeways. Once credited to the Druids, it probably dates from the Neolithic.', 'teaser-card-s024:7f4207f8-30df-4aa9-850e-6873fcab2bb3:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'ba1f27e1a46826c3fd5090ef5d55c2b0ce6fd985daf12febd8fc5e8d978f863d|35c867fa873b2c7fa38835a992daceae68cca1ac705c5537e3ac37ddf6ef44aa|35c867fa873b2c7fa38835a992daceae68cca1ac705c5537e3ac37ddf6ef44aa', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Inside Fairy Toot, a Somerset barrow opened between 1787 and 1835, lay two rows of cells of immense stones set edgeways. Once credited to the Druids, it probably dates from the Neolithic."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fairy Toot is a barrow [S1]; it is in Somerset [S1]; it was opened between 1787 and 1835 [S5]; it contained two rows of cells [S5]; the cells were formed by immense stones set edgeways [S5]; it was once attributed (conjectured) to the Druids [S7]; it probably dates from the Neolithic [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fairy Toot is a barrow (burial mound) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy_Toot]; It is in Somerset [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy_Toot]; It was opened between 1787 and 1835 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy_Toot]; Inside lay two rows of cells [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy_Toot]; The cells were formed of immense stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy_Toot]; The stones were set edgeways [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy_Toot]; It was once credited to the Druids [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy_Toot]; It probably dates from the Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy_Toot]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7f5c1a4a-0004-410a-a275-ecd2e8368867'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Rock-cut caves from the Maurya Empire (322–185 BC) — the oldest surviving rock-cut caves in all of the subcontinent. They were carved for the Ajivika sect.', 'Cavernas de Marabar: in the Barabar hills, Ashoka''s Minor Rock Edicts mark the caves Sudama, Visvakarma and Karna Chopar, and his grandson Dasaratha inscribed three other caves.', 'teaser-card-s024:7f5c1a4a-0004-410a-a275-ecd2e8368867:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 177 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '0a14f777f5565c7243b73e51987c616d8fceb908a941c1cd631d8d73f7d00232|2a49fd662c151dd65f121255ce6481edcfb5ea95f3d7db553eb6e5d36755ab8d|2a49fd662c151dd65f121255ce6481edcfb5ea95f3d7db553eb6e5d36755ab8d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cavernas de Marabar: in the Barabar hills, Ashoka''s Minor Rock Edicts mark the caves Sudama, Visvakarma and Karna Chopar, and his grandson Dasaratha inscribed three other caves."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the caves lie in the Barabar hills [S3]; Ashoka''s inscriptions are Minor Rock Edicts [S2]; the Minor Rock Edict inscriptions appear in the caves Sudama, Visvakarma and Karna Chopar [S2]; Dasaratha was Ashoka''s grandson [S3]; Dasaratha inscribed three other caves [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-004 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cavernas de Marabar (Barabar Caves) are rock-cut caves in the Barabar hills [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barabar_Caves]; Ashoka''s inscriptions in the caves belong to his Minor Rock Edicts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barabar_Caves]; The Ashoka inscriptions appear in the caves Sudama, Visvakarma and Karna Chopar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barabar_Caves]; Dasaratha is Ashoka''s grandson [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barabar_Caves]; Dasaratha inscribed three other caves of the Barabar hills [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barabar_Caves]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7f9f3957-7fc4-4775-8c45-93c279feb753'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Windmill Tump, also known as Rodmarton Chambered Tomb, is a Neolithic burial site, a stone tumulus or barrow.', 'Behind a false entrance on the eastern side of Windmill Tump, a Neolithic cairn of approximately 5,000 tons of stone, two tombs held the remains of ten adults and three children.', 'teaser-card-s024:7f9f3957-7fc4-4775-8c45-93c279feb753:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 178 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8ec03560757f97be832cde2583322ac3455ca33ec2b2861354f4a8cbdf45430d|e452999b0ae654081f597a8fb73ef770d3b8e668a2acd85950077beb76b65499|e452999b0ae654081f597a8fb73ef770d3b8e668a2acd85950077beb76b65499', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Behind a false entrance on the eastern side of Windmill Tump, a Neolithic cairn of approximately 5,000 tons of stone, two tombs held the remains of ten adults and three children."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Windmill Tump has a false entrance [S5, S7]; the false entrance is on the eastern side of the mound [S5]; it is a Neolithic cairn [S1, S2]; it consists of approximately 5,000 tons of stone [S4]; two tombs lie behind the false entrance [S7]; the tombs held the remains of ten adults and three children [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Windmill Tump is a burial site, a stone tumulus or barrow [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]; It is a cairn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]; It is Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]; It consists of approximately 5,000 tons of stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]; There is a false entrance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]; The false entrance is on the eastern side [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]; Two tombs lie behind the false entrance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]; The tombs held ten adults [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]; The tombs held three children [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill_Tump]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7fe4bb17-d008-4886-a097-c03c66b43f76'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Neolithic stalled cairn from 3000 BC with compartmented burial chambers. Its oblong form measures 22.5 metres, typical of the Orkney-Cromarty tradition.', 'Looking out over Eynhallow Sound on Rousay, the Neolithic Blackhammer Chambered Cairn encloses a long burial chamber 13.5 m by 2 m, and 2 m in height, within an oblong tomb.', 'teaser-card-s024:7fe4bb17-d008-4886-a097-c03c66b43f76:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 173 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '134bc59a7fdb7e436e3b4047ed1c7ac08e46b9cb9c01ddadce3d12a1aa2b34bc|430b56da03ed2450ae2d1d114ff571224c12d830c8aa757ebb31b515d8ce78a7|430b56da03ed2450ae2d1d114ff571224c12d830c8aa757ebb31b515d8ce78a7', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Looking out over Eynhallow Sound on Rousay, the Neolithic Blackhammer Chambered Cairn encloses a long burial chamber 13.5 m by 2 m, and 2 m in height, within an oblong tomb."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the cairn looks out over Eynhallow Sound [S3]; it is on Rousay [S1]; it is Neolithic [S1]; it encloses a long burial chamber [S5]; the chamber measures 13.5 m by 2 m [S5]; the chamber is 2 m in height [S5]; the tomb is oblong [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Blackhammer is a chambered cairn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackhammer_Chambered_Cairn]; It is Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackhammer_Chambered_Cairn]; It is on Rousay [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackhammer_Chambered_Cairn]; It looks out over Eynhallow Sound [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackhammer_Chambered_Cairn]; It encloses a long burial chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackhammer_Chambered_Cairn]; The chamber measures 13.5 m by 2 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackhammer_Chambered_Cairn]; The chamber is 2 m in height [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackhammer_Chambered_Cairn]; The chamber lies within an oblong tomb [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackhammer_Chambered_Cairn]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8126681d-e2a2-49f2-ab9a-4695c751ce23'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A pre-Inca cemetery with above-ground chullpa towers that housed entire families. Grave robbers dynamited many, but the remaining towers still stand.', 'On the eastern shore of Lake Umayo stand the chullpas of Sillustani, funerary towers built by highland societies as mausoleums for high-ranking individuals and groups linked by lineage.', 'teaser-card-s024:8126681d-e2a2-49f2-ab9a-4695c751ce23:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '87382173a9a4330334e26599211d72954242c2539dcc3965f0855e4186638ebc|57b8a829f39ac40af6241774f085f059c8feae37d9bd7a17a71b895c8bcf4dad|57b8a829f39ac40af6241774f085f059c8feae37d9bd7a17a71b895c8bcf4dad', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the eastern shore of Lake Umayo stand the chullpas of Sillustani, funerary towers built by highland societies as mausoleums for high-ranking individuals and groups linked by lineage."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sillustani stands on the eastern shore of Lake Umayo [S3]; Sillustani has chullpas [S2]; the chullpas are funerary towers built by highland societies [S2]; the chullpas were mausoleums for high-ranking individuals [S6]; the chullpas were mausoleums for groups linked by lineage [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sillustani is a funerary complex [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sillustani]; Its chullpas are funerary towers [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sillustani]; It lies on the eastern shore of Lake Umayo [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sillustani]; The towers were built by highland societies [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sillustani]; The chullpas were mausoleums for high-ranking individuals [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sillustani]; and for groups linked by lineage [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sillustani]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('81724be4-6588-41b1-8efc-364637456cdf'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 110-meter-long horse figure from ~1000 BC, carved by filling trenches with crushed white chalk. It must be re-scoured every few decades or the grass reclaims it.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s024:81724be4-6588-41b1-8efc-364637456cdf:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (card-clear-unproven-after-verify): card cleared (unproven-after-verify)', '||a599d7655c16257dbf7822482579633734fa1d3f5273526e30f911d7f6b07515', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: unproven-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: The Uffington White Horse is a prehistoric hill figure (a chalk figure cut into a hillside) on Whitehorse Hill in England; The web check could not prove: The figure is 110 m long; The web check could not prove: Uffington White Horse is a prehistoric hill figure; The web check could not prove: The figure is formed of deep trenches; The web check could not prove: The trenches are filled with crushed white chalk"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8325d125-ed63-4a02-8c93-1e2a6683f5e1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'One of Europe''s best-preserved mithraea, this underground temple to the sun god Mithra imitates a cave. It is now protected inside a glass-walled cage.', 'Found by chance in 1931 during digging for a house, the Jajce Mithraeum is a temple of Mithraism from the early 4th century, perhaps even the 2nd, and among Europe''s best-preserved sites.', 'teaser-card-s024:8325d125-ed63-4a02-8c93-1e2a6683f5e1:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '44fd1a3cb3f87979915b05f4fb9bced691fd5f4c807bc183ef914fb40e435e31|888623c1c0d05cb7f7302952acadd3c39000ec8766a71f2d521bbf5b2bed7fae|888623c1c0d05cb7f7302952acadd3c39000ec8766a71f2d521bbf5b2bed7fae', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Found by chance in 1931 during digging for a house, the Jajce Mithraeum is a temple of Mithraism from the early 4th century, perhaps even the 2nd, and among Europe''s best-preserved sites."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it was found by chance [S5]; it was found in 1931 [S5]; it was found during digging for a house [S5]; the Jajce Mithraeum is a temple of Mithraism [S1]; it dates from the early 4th century [S2]; it is perhaps as old as the 2nd century (hedged) [S2]; it is among the best-preserved sites in Europe [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Jajce Mithraeum is a temple of Mithraism (in Jajce, Bosnia and Herzegovina) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jajce_Mithraeum]; It was found by chance (accidentally) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jajce_Mithraeum]; It was found in 1931 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jajce_Mithraeum]; It was found during digging for a house [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jajce_Mithraeum]; It dates from the early 4th century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jajce_Mithraeum]; It may even date from the 2nd century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jajce_Mithraeum]; It is among Europe''s best-preserved sites [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jajce_Mithraeum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('835c08e0-d141-483d-b193-017a13a0e458'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Maya highland site identified as Jakawitz, the legendary first capital of the K''iche'' Maya. It spans the Early Classic to Late Postclassic on just 2 hectares.', 'Chitinamit, identified as Jakawitz, first capital of the K''iche'' Maya, lay behind a stone rampart. Its end seems to have been violent: many projectile points, traces of burned buildings.', 'teaser-card-s024:835c08e0-d141-483d-b193-017a13a0e458:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'd41e16bf46bf7d931711524abf97a24bb2613213cd8976aaf9ade9abe871c6ff|ab7f6d826ef6134eaae5f67e9af17714943d77fc69ff53d3163017ebe4f7267f|ab7f6d826ef6134eaae5f67e9af17714943d77fc69ff53d3163017ebe4f7267f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chitinamit, identified as Jakawitz, first capital of the K''iche'' Maya, lay behind a stone rampart. Its end seems to have been violent: many projectile points, traces of burned buildings."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chitinamit has been identified as Jakawitz [S2]; Jakawitz was the first capital of the K''iche'' Maya [S2]; the site lay behind a stone rampart [S6]; its end seems to have been violent (hedged) [S7]; many projectile points were found [S7]; there are traces of burned buildings [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chitinamit is an archaeological site of the Maya civilization in the highlands of Guatemala [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chitinamit]; Chitinamit has been identified as Jakawitz, the first capital of the K''iche'' Maya [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chitinamit]; The site lay behind (was defended by) a stone rampart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chitinamit]; Its end seems to have been violent [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chitinamit]; Many projectile points were found [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chitinamit]; There are traces of burned buildings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chitinamit]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8424266d-c9ed-42b0-b04c-004e21cd06de'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Roman ala fort near Weissenburg on the Upper Germanic-Rhaetian Limes, now a World Heritage component. Partly subterranean ruins and a reconstructed bathhouse complex survive at the site.', 'At Weissenburg, near the Roman limes, the fort once called Biriciana keeps a reconstructed north gateway, large thermal baths and building remains lying partly underground.', 'teaser-card-s024:8424266d-c9ed-42b0-b04c-004e21cd06de:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 172 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '827897027a4c414255807d23f0b8ac126d1b33eb703899f0d936ab7fbd8d6ed2|20cb7c9b514d137f3f87d40de6cab3bd81b56d00a4db3ef1233a95f668bf3541|20cb7c9b514d137f3f87d40de6cab3bd81b56d00a4db3ef1233a95f668bf3541', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Weissenburg, near the Roman limes, the fort once called Biriciana keeps a reconstructed north gateway, large thermal baths and building remains lying partly underground."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the fort is at Weissenburg [S1, S2]; it lies near the Roman limes [S1]; the fort was once called Biriciana [S1]; it has a reconstructed north gateway [S4]; it has large thermal baths [S4]; it has building remains lying partly underground [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Biriciana is a Roman fort (castellum) at Weissenburg, Germany [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biriciana]; The fort lies near the Roman limes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biriciana]; It was once called Biriciana [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biriciana]; The site keeps a reconstructed north gateway [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biriciana]; The site has large thermal baths [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biriciana]; Building remains lie partly underground [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biriciana]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('84f1b5af-954a-4b0a-aa4f-7911bb2adc74'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Clifftop fort at 140 metres on the Devon coast, part of the Jurassic Coast World Heritage Site. Coastal erosion steadily consumes the ramparts -- the sea is slowly reclaiming the site.', 'On a plateau near the cliff edge at Branscombe, the Iron Age hillfort of Berry Camp still shows three sides of bank and ditch. The fourth is thought lost to coastal erosion.', 'teaser-card-s024:84f1b5af-954a-4b0a-aa4f-7911bb2adc74:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 173 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '371aebed81d1dd164d578ea641c698b4b61e4c4105489701a2c6f3b5f0440a91|23adf853b29c289c36ab9734771572bc3b09680798cea787890fc2362c53f235|23adf853b29c289c36ab9734771572bc3b09680798cea787890fc2362c53f235', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a plateau near the cliff edge at Branscombe, the Iron Age hillfort of Berry Camp still shows three sides of bank and ditch. The fourth is thought lost to coastal erosion."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Berry Camp lies on a plateau [S1]; it lies near the cliff edge [S1]; it is at Branscombe [S1]; it is a hillfort [S1]; it dates to the Iron Age [S3]; three sides of bank and ditch are still visible [S4]; a fourth side is thought to have been lost to coastal erosion (hedged) [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Berry Camp is a hillfort at Branscombe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Camp]; The hillfort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Camp]; It stands on a plateau [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Camp]; It is near the cliff edge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Camp]; It is at Branscombe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Camp]; Three sides of bank and ditch are still visible [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Camp]; The fourth side is thought lost to coastal erosion [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Camp]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('855cc936-929b-4e3f-87fa-cef41ed81ca0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A ceremonial center from 1200 to 500 BC built with cut and polished stone. A dozen related archaeological sites surround it in the northern highlands.', 'At Pacopampa, a ceremonial center of cut and polished stone, archaeologists found a deep, boot-shaped tomb free from looters: a woman, presumably of great power, from around 900 BC.', 'teaser-card-s024:855cc936-929b-4e3f-87fa-cef41ed81ca0:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '49bb1537b98cd80bb008f1724d90818209bc49fd49dce4ad185de03e9ba3e732|11304dae1445c0e3f45ff3af59302af271f6698f770c41941d75edb292b7f3fd|11304dae1445c0e3f45ff3af59302af271f6698f770c41941d75edb292b7f3fd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Pacopampa, a ceremonial center of cut and polished stone, archaeologists found a deep, boot-shaped tomb free from looters: a woman, presumably of great power, from around 900 BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pacopampa is a ceremonial center [S2]; it is built of cut and polished stone [S2]; archaeologists found a tomb there [S4]; the tomb is deep [S5]; the tomb is boot-shaped [S5]; the tomb was free from looters [S5]; the tomb held a woman [S4]; she was presumably a person of great power (hedged) [S4]; the burial dates from around 900 BC [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pacopampa is a ceremonial center [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; The ceremonial center is made of cut stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; The ceremonial center is made of polished stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; Archaeologists found a tomb at Pacopampa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; The tomb is shaped like a boot [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; The tomb is deep [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; The tomb was free from looters [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; The tomb holds a woman presumably of great power [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; The tomb dates from around 900 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]; Pacopampa is in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacopampa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('856fe292-38b8-433d-8cde-052863c98659'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman vicus from the 1st century BC whose remains lie beneath Oberwinterthur in Zürich canton. Excavations around the medieval St. Arbogast church have uncovered commercial and religious buildings.', 'In Oberwinterthur lie the remains of Vitudurum, a Roman vicus founded near a prehistoric route from Lake Geneva to Lake Constance. Neolithic and Bronze Age remains came to light there too.', 'teaser-card-s024:856fe292-38b8-433d-8cde-052863c98659:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'da22968bb5f0e2f09c7c51681836d63bc5f54da96f8332a63df41bff87c5d19d|c1118cb6f279b4f57e5bb412d9dba679c070726276a7ed477e04129ecd6aa958|c1118cb6f279b4f57e5bb412d9dba679c070726276a7ed477e04129ecd6aa958', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Oberwinterthur lie the remains of Vitudurum, a Roman vicus founded near a prehistoric route from Lake Geneva to Lake Constance. Neolithic and Bronze Age remains came to light there too."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the remains of Vitudurum lie in Oberwinterthur [S1]; Vitudurum was a Roman vicus [S1]; it was founded near a prehistoric route from Lake Geneva to Lake Constance [S2]; Neolithic remains were uncovered there [S5]; Bronze Age remains were uncovered there [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vitudurum was a Roman vicus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitudurum]; Its remains lie in Oberwinterthur [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitudurum]; The vicus was founded near a prehistoric route [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitudurum]; The prehistoric route ran from Lake Geneva to Lake Constance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitudurum]; Neolithic remains came to light in the area [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitudurum]; Bronze Age remains came to light in the area [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitudurum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8571f9c3-159d-4d6d-b33a-3e36fdabb5cf'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 3rd-century temple to Mithras with the classic layout: a sunken central nave flanked by raised benches where initiates reclined during secret rituals.', 'Partly hollowed out of the natural rock, the Fertőrákos Mithraeum is a temple to the god Mithras: a sunken nave between podium benches, built roughly at the start of the 3rd century AD.', 'teaser-card-s024:8571f9c3-159d-4d6d-b33a-3e36fdabb5cf:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '653ab54edb97a9ed2c724b6f94fc19c2198ba1d2f1f24e27efe30b587ef5a36e|4864eabb159af8c56f400a93681b3e95286c5ad57a8b82abf4e9e5d1f922dc23|4864eabb159af8c56f400a93681b3e95286c5ad57a8b82abf4e9e5d1f922dc23', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Partly hollowed out of the natural rock, the Fertőrákos Mithraeum is a temple to the god Mithras: a sunken nave between podium benches, built roughly at the start of the 3rd century AD."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Mithraeum is partly hollowed out of the natural rock [S6]; it is a temple to the god Mithras [S1]; it has a sunken nave [S2]; the nave lies between podium benches [S2]; it was built roughly at the start of the 3rd century AD (hedged) [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Fertőrákos Mithraeum is a temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fert%C5%91r%C3%A1kos_mithraeum]; It is dedicated to the god Mithras [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fert%C5%91r%C3%A1kos_mithraeum]; It is partly hollowed out of the natural rock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fert%C5%91r%C3%A1kos_mithraeum]; It has a sunken nave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fert%C5%91r%C3%A1kos_mithraeum]; The nave lies between podium benches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fert%C5%91r%C3%A1kos_mithraeum]; It was built roughly at the start of the 3rd century AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fert%C5%91r%C3%A1kos_mithraeum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('85ed945a-9d3c-437c-97b5-adf0cf0b3a9c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Cave at 2,000 metres in Styria with bone tools dated 65,000–31,000 BC — Middle Paleolithic tools found alongside Neolithic hearths. Discovered by hunters sheltering from a storm in 1924.', 'In 1924 two hunters sheltering from a storm in Salzofen Cave found fossil bones. They gave them to a school principal, who began digging the next day, unearthing large amounts of fossils.', 'teaser-card-s024:85ed945a-9d3c-437c-97b5-adf0cf0b3a9c:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'c81a08a94302ab7f6a17f422139e77200619ec73ccb4bdec8a90ebdb77374136|415148842f74e0a3104e4ddab92ebd3bf893146322519d512901bc3df36c748e|415148842f74e0a3104e4ddab92ebd3bf893146322519d512901bc3df36c748e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite1-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1924 two hunters sheltering from a storm in Salzofen Cave found fossil bones. They gave them to a school principal, who began digging the next day, unearthing large amounts of fossils."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check1-001 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "in 1924 two hunters found fossil bones [S2]; the hunters were sheltering from a storm [S2]; they were sheltering in Salzofen Cave [S2]; the hunters gave the bones to a school principal [S3]; the principal began digging the next day [S4]; the digging unearthed large amounts of fossils [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Salzofen Cave is a cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salzofen_Cave]; The discovery was in 1924 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salzofen_Cave]; Two hunters found fossil bones in the cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salzofen_Cave]; The hunters were sheltering from a storm [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salzofen_Cave]; They gave the bones to a school principal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salzofen_Cave]; The principal began digging the next day [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salzofen_Cave]; He unearthed large amounts of fossils [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salzofen_Cave]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8797faec-8590-4716-9763-310be93ef6ea'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A four-story sandstone tower tomb built in 103 AD outside Palmyra''s city walls. Tower tombs like this could hold hundreds of burials in stacked compartments.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s024:8797faec-8590-4716-9763-310be93ef6ea:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (card-clear-unproven-after-verify): card cleared (unproven-after-verify)', '||b2f22feb90539ef295224d2e23d88bf09d4e758085e241696c4d7da5fbeb55b8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: unproven-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: The Tower of Elahbel is a tomb; The web check could not prove: It was a four-storey tower; The web check could not prove: It was built of sandstone; The web check could not prove: Tower of Elahbel is a tower tomb near Palmyra, Syria"}]'::jsonb),
    ('87abc0f0-0a56-420f-94ce-ada2998bbd0d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The cave consists of a narrow passage about 13 metres long and one metre wide, with a chamber at the end that is about two metres wide.', 'A narrow passage about 13 metres long ends in a chamber: the Cave of Pego do Diabo, which held bones of humans together with those of wolves, bears, hyenas and the Iberian lynx.', 'teaser-card-s024:87abc0f0-0a56-420f-94ce-ada2998bbd0d:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 177 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'a00246ff589b6073943eb4730f977250a1904ce4b661ea03d23aed25e3c4c2ae|b6713474aa33c6985ae52342ec333c62ccd7f63d2278e23636fc8cd300338f32|b6713474aa33c6985ae52342ec333c62ccd7f63d2278e23636fc8cd300338f32', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A narrow passage about 13 metres long ends in a chamber: the Cave of Pego do Diabo, which held bones of humans together with those of wolves, bears, hyenas and the Iberian lynx."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the cave has a narrow passage [S2]; the passage is about 13 metres long [S2]; the passage ends in a chamber [S2]; the Cave of Pego do Diabo held human bones [S7]; it held bones of wolves, bears, hyenas and the Iberian lynx together with the human bones [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Cave of Pego do Diabo is a cave in Portugal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_Pego_do_Diabo]; The cave has a narrow passage about 13 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_Pego_do_Diabo]; The passage ends in a chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_Pego_do_Diabo]; The cave held bones of humans together with those of wolves, bears and hyenas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_Pego_do_Diabo]; The cave held bones of the Iberian lynx [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_Pego_do_Diabo]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('88897642-cf8a-48c2-a9a4-ebb0408a8804'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Innisidgen is the site of two Bronze Age entrance graves on the island of St Mary''s in the Isles of Scilly.', 'On a steep slope above Crow Sound, Innisidgen keeps two Bronze Age entrance graves. The upper, known as the Giant''s Grave, holds an intact burial chamber under a mound of earth and rubble.', 'teaser-card-s024:88897642-cf8a-48c2-a9a4-ebb0408a8804:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '71fba4f027b5b33c02b17c167847409250ef29b10576097addaa5b1ced12f92a|621e172b326a25cfd7b62882e17464e5ae90a6b9b224dc8fb3e5b71cd2acfb8a|621e172b326a25cfd7b62882e17464e5ae90a6b9b224dc8fb3e5b71cd2acfb8a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a steep slope above Crow Sound, Innisidgen keeps two Bronze Age entrance graves. The upper, known as the Giant''s Grave, holds an intact burial chamber under a mound of earth and rubble."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the graves lie on a steep slope [S2]; the slope is above Crow Sound [S2]; Innisidgen has two entrance graves [S1]; the graves date to the Bronze Age [S1]; the upper grave is known as the Giant''s Grave [S6]; the upper grave holds an intact burial chamber [S8]; the chamber lies under a mound of earth and rubble [S7, S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Innisidgen is a site of burial monuments (entrance graves) on St Mary''s, Isles of Scilly, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innisidgen]; There are two entrance graves at Innisidgen [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innisidgen]; The graves date to the Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innisidgen]; The graves lie on a steep slope [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innisidgen]; The graves are above/overlooking Crow Sound [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innisidgen]; The upper grave is known as the Giant''s Grave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innisidgen]; The upper grave holds an intact burial chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innisidgen]; The upper grave''s chamber lies under a mound of earth and rubble [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innisidgen]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('898f67da-9f3f-46ca-bc0d-d91d1d58ae45'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Karakoram Highway crossing used by traders, pilgrims, and armies for centuries. Sasanian-era rock carvings left by passing travellers make it one of the best-documented ancient crossroads in Asia.', 'Traders, Buddhist missionaries, Sogdian merchants and pilgrims passed through Shatial and wrote on its rocks. At the bridge on the Indus: more than 1000 inscriptions and 700 petroglyphs.', 'teaser-card-s024:898f67da-9f3f-46ca-bc0d-d91d1d58ae45:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9ae44df53ba15a2109086945c28d162176d3a62bfec5047d3647f05c9fff6ff3|e7bfd4777b92e94ac2cef35e3462d9f4b0285f58f4ab9e95eb91b146b924e6a1|e7bfd4777b92e94ac2cef35e3462d9f4b0285f58f4ab9e95eb91b146b924e6a1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Traders, Buddhist missionaries, Sogdian merchants and pilgrims passed through Shatial and wrote on its rocks. At the bridge on the Indus: more than 1000 inscriptions and 700 petroglyphs."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "traders passed through Shatial [S1]; Buddhist missionaries passed through Shatial [S1]; Sogdian merchants passed through Shatial [S1]; pilgrims passed through Shatial [S1]; they left writing (graffiti and inscriptions) on its rocks [S1]; there is a bridge on the Indus at Shatial [S2]; more than 1000 inscriptions are found at the bridge [S2]; 700 petroglyphs are found at the bridge [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Shatial is a transit station with archaeological significance (rock inscription site) in northern Pakistan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]; Traders passed through Shatial [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]; Buddhist missionaries passed through Shatial [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]; Sogdian merchants passed through Shatial [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]; Pilgrims passed through Shatial [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]; These travellers wrote/left inscriptions on the rocks of Shatial [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]; There is a bridge on the Indus at Shatial where the rock art is found [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]; More than 1000 inscriptions are found at the Shatial bridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]; 700 petroglyphs are found at the Shatial bridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatial]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a082425-b949-4e34-a37a-02238d6a2817'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The fort, built in the first century BC, was constructed atop a natural plateau rising over 400 m above the surrounding terrain.', 'Above the Dead Sea, Masada National Park holds Herod the Great''s palace on three terraces of the northern cliff. It became the final holdout of Jewish rebels as Romans built a massive ramp.', 'teaser-card-s024:8a082425-b949-4e34-a37a-02238d6a2817:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9752e18e3055463b11dcd5c1a7e0e7b0a617646c1d778ebc688cd6c39d7f32d6|1f3c1552097ed7e3cef064409a66580c35a11d106265b13c720c45ad05247e51|1f3c1552097ed7e3cef064409a66580c35a11d106265b13c720c45ad05247e51', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Above the Dead Sea, Masada National Park holds Herod the Great''s palace on three terraces of the northern cliff. It became the final holdout of Jewish rebels as Romans built a massive ramp."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Masada lies above the Dead Sea [S1]; Herod the Great built a palace there [S3, S4]; that palace spans three terraces on the northern cliff [S4]; it became the final holdout of Jewish rebels [S5]; the Romans built a massive ramp against it [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Masada is a mountain-top fortress complex overlooking the Dead Sea in Israel [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masada]; Herod the Great built a palace at Masada [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masada]; The northern palace stands on three terraces of the northern cliff [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masada]; Masada became the final holdout of Jewish rebels [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masada]; The Romans built a massive ramp during the siege [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masada]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a66f7db-94df-45d9-919f-760b966192be'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'On an extinct volcano in West Java, 370 andesite steps climb to the Gunung Padang Megalithic Site: five terraces, each higher than the last, walled and covered with volcanic stone columns.', 'teaser-card-s024:8a66f7db-94df-45d9-919f-760b966192be:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '64354db277f4ac8e153edecf80aff10070e5994033aff51b94877ee3d3d434cc|db4e933d75a1e067f13afeaf22ba610fe870c7e725bc86c567f9263fdff52544|db4e933d75a1e067f13afeaf22ba610fe870c7e725bc86c567f9263fdff52544', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On an extinct volcano in West Java, 370 andesite steps climb to the Gunung Padang Megalithic Site: five terraces, each higher than the last, walled and covered with volcanic stone columns."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies on an extinct volcano [S2]; it is in West Java [S1]; 370 steps climb to the site [S2]; the steps are of andesite [S2]; the site has five terraces [S2, S4]; each terrace is higher than the last [S4]; the terraces are walled [S2, S7]; the walls are made of volcanic stone columns [S7]; the site is covered with volcanic stone columns [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gunung Padang is a megalithic archaeological site (terraced complex) in Indonesia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]; The site is in West Java [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]; The site lies on an extinct volcano [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]; There are 370 steps up to the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]; The steps are of andesite [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]; The site has five terraces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]; Each terrace is higher than the last [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]; The terraces are walled [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]; The site is covered with volcanic stone columns [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunung_Padang_Megalithic_Site]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a93ea1c-7dc4-4b11-b68e-39524fa0b8bc'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III is a black limestone Neo-Assyrian sculpture with many scenes in bas-relief and inscriptions.', 'In 825 BC, amid civil war, the Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III rose in the central square of Nimrud: black limestone carved with scenes of tribute and the king''s annals in cuneiform.', 'teaser-card-s024:8a93ea1c-7dc4-4b11-b68e-39524fa0b8bc:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 183 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '1800306ab3fc08675d191d789c8cd6594524ca2f662e28e692a2e0f553fefeba|06b46f0a3f00b19216cb1a5570343cf3a53344930b9f301dc42b9298d4003237|06b46f0a3f00b19216cb1a5570343cf3a53344930b9f301dc42b9298d4003237', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 825 BC, amid civil war, the Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III rose in the central square of Nimrud: black limestone carved with scenes of tribute and the king''s annals in cuneiform."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the obelisk was erected in 825 BC [S4]; it was erected at a time of civil war [S4]; it stood in the central square of Nimrud [S4]; it is made of black limestone [S1]; it is carved with scenes in relief [S1]; the scenes show tribute [S3]; it bears the king''s annals [S6]; the annals are written in cuneiform [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III is a monument/sculpture from Nimrud [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]; Nimrud, where it stood, is in Iraq [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]; It was erected in 825 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]; It was erected at a time of civil war [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]; It stood in the central square of Nimrud [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]; It is made of black limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]; It is carved with scenes of tribute [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]; It carries the annals of the king [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]; The annals are in cuneiform script [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8aa48093-66ab-456b-bbd6-b2ba884d47ed'::uuid, 'card_description', '6th century BC-1st century AD Pre-Columbian Maya city in the Petén Basin, peak population ~10,000. Located 40 km east of Tikal on a ridge above the Holmul River.', 'Long lost in the jungle of the Petén, Cival hid temples on step pyramids, plazas pointing to the equinox sunrise and stucco sculptures of deities, until looters found it around 1980.', 'teaser-card-s024:8aa48093-66ab-456b-bbd6-b2ba884d47ed:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '66a0ac5d9d72ee79eeca80f90e764ddbb7331dd87268c7846ba1dac20bcfd910|5bb0523bcfa1402398b5cf4366b1a7e23d09dd5a7d6923afceb513bcbfe6016a|5bb0523bcfa1402398b5cf4366b1a7e23d09dd5a7d6923afceb513bcbfe6016a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Long lost in the jungle of the Petén, Cival hid temples on step pyramids, plazas pointing to the equinox sunrise and stucco sculptures of deities, until looters found it around 1980."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cival was long lost in the jungle [S7]; it is in the Petén [S1]; it has temples on step pyramids [S4]; it has plazas arranged to point to the equinox sunrise [S4]; it has stucco sculptures depicting deities [S6]; it stayed hidden until looters found it [S7]; looters found it around 1980 [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cival is an archaeological site, formerly a major Maya city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cival]; Cival is in the Petén, Guatemala [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cival]; Cival was long lost in the jungle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cival]; It has temples on step pyramids [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cival]; Its plazas point to the equinox sunrise [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cival]; Some buildings had stucco sculptures of deities [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cival]; Looters found it around 1980 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cival]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8b190e2c-f9ea-4d46-803a-11750b5119ba'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Third-millennium BC copper smelting community in Huelva, achieving furnace temperatures of 1,200 degrees Celsius. It provides very early evidence of copper metallurgy in Atlantic Europe.', 'Excavations at Cabezo Juré in Alosno, Andalusia, have laid bare the vestiges of an ancient community of workers specialized in copper metallurgy, active between 2873 and 2274 BC.', 'teaser-card-s024:8b190e2c-f9ea-4d46-803a-11750b5119ba:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 178 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'af34807907bc4ad5002719311ee70291da7638060735e0aec89c2dc694194bd1|4262e7650d7fc254bd98c68c764840d64bdb9c0a1462fa23edef51352570f517|4262e7650d7fc254bd98c68c764840d64bdb9c0a1462fa23edef51352570f517', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Excavations at Cabezo Juré in Alosno, Andalusia, have laid bare the vestiges of an ancient community of workers specialized in copper metallurgy, active between 2873 and 2274 BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cabezo Jure is in Alosno [S1]; Alosno is in Andalusia [S1]; excavations revealed the vestiges of an ancient community of workers [S2]; the workers were specialized in copper metallurgy [S2]; it was active between 2873 and 2274 BC [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cabezo Juré is an archaeological site in Alosno [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabezo_Jur%C3%A9]; Alosno is in Andalusia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabezo_Jur%C3%A9]; Excavations revealed vestiges of an ancient community of workers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabezo_Jur%C3%A9]; The workers specialized in copper metallurgy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabezo_Jur%C3%A9]; Radiocarbon dating puts activity between 2873 and 2274 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabezo_Jur%C3%A9]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8ca3f6a9-2b4a-461e-8f64-84b43eb87201'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Archaeological remains include house complexes, some of which resemble the megaron-type building with central hearth, and a "Temple" or sanctuary, where votive figures were found.', 'High in the Dikti Mountains of Crete, crowned by a limestone knob locals call the nail, lies Karfi, a Minoan town of paved streets and a shrine with an altar. Its ancient name is unknown.', 'teaser-card-s024:8ca3f6a9-2b4a-461e-8f64-84b43eb87201:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'bba69c7524e77fa66b6ae5c90d8710f8bf1941d246c06709744cc644af5a9f8f|efc4992296de121d8c141ed3dbee34512fc300633b96f2beb513e02a9232edc8|efc4992296de121d8c141ed3dbee34512fc300633b96f2beb513e02a9232edc8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "High in the Dikti Mountains of Crete, crowned by a limestone knob locals call the nail, lies Karfi, a Minoan town of paved streets and a shrine with an altar. Its ancient name is unknown."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Karfi lies high in the Dikti Mountains [S1, S3]; it is on Crete [S1]; a limestone knob marks the top of the site [S2]; locals call the knob by a name meaning ''the nail'' [S2]; Karfi was a Minoan town [S6]; the town had paved streets [S6]; the town had a shrine with an altar [S6]; its ancient name is unknown [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Karfi is an archaeological site in the Dikti Mountains of Crete [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karfi]; The site lies high up in the mountains [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karfi]; Karfi is a Minoan town [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karfi]; Karfi means the nail [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karfi]; A limestone knob crowns the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karfi]; The town has paved streets [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karfi]; The town has a shrine with an altar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karfi]; Its ancient name is unknown [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karfi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8dace4e6-f6f7-4901-8132-897017b01700'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 2,500-year-old city with four distinct archaeological layers from Hindu, Buddhist, Muslim, and Sikh civilizations stacked on top of each other.', 'More than 2,500 years old, Tulamba hides four layers, Hindu, Buddhist, Muslim and Sikh. When Alexander the Great invaded, the Moi people who ruled it fought his forces.', 'teaser-card-s024:8dace4e6-f6f7-4901-8132-897017b01700:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 168 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '1cc78dc49aa80fc983f0dc9083e79200a4b1ab7c5f3624a247c8aed2fa8a14a9|34f34d33dbe8d9890c93b106785b3c048d8a15441f3a5f233e63f9ae07406c08|34f34d33dbe8d9890c93b106785b3c048d8a15441f3a5f233e63f9ae07406c08', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "More than 2,500 years old, Tulamba hides four layers, Hindu, Buddhist, Muslim and Sikh. When Alexander the Great invaded, the Moi people who ruled it fought his forces."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tulamba is more than 2,500 years old [S1]; it holds four layers [S2]; the layers are Hindu, Buddhist, Muslim and Sikh [S2]; Alexander the Great came to Tulamba when he invaded [S5]; at that time the Moi people ruled Tulamba [S6]; the Moi fought Alexander''s forces [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tulamba is a town in Punjab, Pakistan with ancient ruins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulamba]; Tulamba is more than 2,500 years old [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulamba]; Digs uncovered four layers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulamba]; The four layers are Hindu, Buddhist, Muslim and Sikh [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulamba]; Alexander the Great invaded and came to Tulamba [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulamba]; Tulamba was then ruled by the Moi people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulamba]; The Moi fought Alexander''s forces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulamba]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8dd50bbd-b629-4c5f-8214-647324df6fce'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The mills operated from the beginning of the 2nd century until about the end of the 3rd century.', 'Near Arles, 16 water wheels in two sets of eight descended a steep hillside: the Barbegal Aqueduct and Mills, a Roman complex with an estimated capacity of 4.5 tonnes of flour per day.', 'teaser-card-s024:8dd50bbd-b629-4c5f-8214-647324df6fce:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'bb6231b2fbf58ceadfcc44a32abad88c23beafeae48a0c4bc27e1db13d27ba64|1457052cb714f81398ae50b4fb93262861b2552845296bea864a44842dd45d56|1457052cb714f81398ae50b4fb93262861b2552845296bea864a44842dd45d56', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Arles, 16 water wheels in two sets of eight descended a steep hillside: the Barbegal Aqueduct and Mills, a Roman complex with an estimated capacity of 4.5 tonnes of flour per day."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is near Arles [S1, S2]; it had 16 water wheels [S3]; the wheels were in two sets of eight [S3]; the wheels descended a steep hillside [S3]; it was a Roman complex [S1]; its capacity is estimated at 4.5 tonnes of flour per day [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-050 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The site is a Roman watermill complex (aqueduct and mills) near Arles, France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbegal_aqueduct_and_mills]; The site lies near Arles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbegal_aqueduct_and_mills]; It had 16 water wheels [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbegal_aqueduct_and_mills]; The wheels were in two sets of eight [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbegal_aqueduct_and_mills]; The wheels descended a steep hillside [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbegal_aqueduct_and_mills]; Estimated capacity of 4.5 tonnes of flour per day [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbegal_aqueduct_and_mills]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8ee9e59b-a2fd-4a46-b3a6-a9ae4efc0500'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The temple is 66 m long, 35 m wide and 31 m high, making it only slightly smaller than the Temple of Jupiter.', 'In the Beqaa Valley, the Temple of Bacchus rises 31 m, one of the best preserved Roman temple ruins. Its age is unknown, but its fine ornamentation can be dated to the second century CE.', 'teaser-card-s024:8ee9e59b-a2fd-4a46-b3a6-a9ae4efc0500:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'e417b2c00a05074adcda845ca14aac9deb122ad94d7a38c1063c0caeba94c338|543d00f6eae6f92993690f5a2783f5156ff57db6c803c198e464ea7e09264132|543d00f6eae6f92993690f5a2783f5156ff57db6c803c198e464ea7e09264132', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Beqaa Valley, the Temple of Bacchus rises 31 m, one of the best preserved Roman temple ruins. Its age is unknown, but its fine ornamentation can be dated to the second century CE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the temple is in the Beqaa Valley [S1]; the temple is 31 m high [S8]; it is one of the best preserved Roman temple ruins [S3]; its age is unknown [S3]; its fine ornamentation can be dated to the second century CE [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-050 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Temple of Bacchus is a Roman temple ruin in the Beqaa Valley (Baalbek), Lebanon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Bacchus]; It is 31 m high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Bacchus]; It is one of the best preserved Roman temple ruins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Bacchus]; Its age is unknown [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Bacchus]; Its fine ornamentation can be dated to the second century CE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Bacchus]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8ef67a96-2590-4382-b575-4a15f7c468fe'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Karst cave near Oliva where the most complete Neanderthal skeleton ever found on the Iberian Peninsula was excavated in 2010. Archaeological work has continued here since 1977.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s024:8ef67a96-2590-4382-b575-4a15f7c468fe:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (card-clear-unproven-after-verify): card cleared (unproven-after-verify)', '||e1a12dd3cd758995eae5450098b5ae2816cdf2c106c8b4a83a099fa42f329f40', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: unproven-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: Cova Foradà is a karst cave (archaeological site with Neanderthal remains) in Spain; The web check could not prove: Cova Foradà is a cave with Neanderthal remains near Oliva; The web check could not prove: Oliva is in the Province of Valencia"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8fbbcbd3-8f18-471d-8515-0960e2e093b7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The aqueducts ensured the supply of water to the city of Nazca and the surrounding fields, allowing the cultivation of cotton, beans, potatoes, and other crops in an arid region.', '3 kilometres east of Nazca, the Nazca culture built the Cantalloc Aqueducts, more than 40 of them, used all year round to water fields of cotton, beans and potatoes in an arid region.', 'teaser-card-s024:8fbbcbd3-8f18-471d-8515-0960e2e093b7:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 183 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8e6d166e1aac0f3e409beca6524cf891ab77b060bca5c3ea223e117c1aa2047d|3de1e886ee408ca09e1e4a7d4b000fe3f32a9e6983e3c3f749c3d67298a6b5f7|3de1e886ee408ca09e1e4a7d4b000fe3f32a9e6983e3c3f749c3d67298a6b5f7', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "3 kilometres east of Nazca, the Nazca culture built the Cantalloc Aqueducts, more than 40 of them, used all year round to water fields of cotton, beans and potatoes in an arid region."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the aqueducts are 3 kilometres east of Nazca [S1]; they were built by the Nazca culture [S1]; more than 40 aqueducts were built [S2]; they were used all year round [S2]; they supplied water to fields [S3]; the fields grew cotton, beans and potatoes [S3]; the region is arid [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-050 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Nazca culture built the aqueducts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantalloc_Aqueducts]; The Cantalloc Aqueducts are a series of aqueducts near Nazca, Peru, 3 km east of Nazca [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantalloc_Aqueducts]; More than 40 of them were built [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantalloc_Aqueducts]; They were used all year round [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantalloc_Aqueducts]; They supplied water to the surrounding fields [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantalloc_Aqueducts]; The fields grew cotton, beans and potatoes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantalloc_Aqueducts]; The region is arid [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantalloc_Aqueducts]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('904babe6-e678-4a59-be5f-89228d5cbb59'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the 5th century BC, the Areopagos council sometimes met in the Stoa as well and a rope would be set up to keep people from interrupting its proceedings.', 'In the northwestern corner of the Athenian Agora stood Stoa Basileios, among the smallest known Greek stoas, yet it kept Athens'' laws and the stone on which new magistrates swore their oath.', 'teaser-card-s024:904babe6-e678-4a59-be5f-89228d5cbb59:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 190 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'a846a675ac607bfae682ea1c22e3228f8f7fc6a90aa36afa33455dee8e4ba771|2cb23c702aeb2d4c1c135ba55c6a2f7b326aac6d4e8bc7aceb52781ae12aa619|2cb23c702aeb2d4c1c135ba55c6a2f7b326aac6d4e8bc7aceb52781ae12aa619', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the northwestern corner of the Athenian Agora stood Stoa Basileios, among the smallest known Greek stoas, yet it kept Athens'' laws and the stone on which new magistrates swore their oath."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the stoa stood in the northwestern corner of the Athenian Agora [S1]; it is among the smallest known Greek stoas [S2]; it was the repository of Athens'' laws [S2]; it held the stone on which incoming magistrates swore their oath [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-050 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Stoa Basileios was a stoa in the northwestern corner of the Athenian Agora [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Basileios]; It is among the smallest known Greek stoas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Basileios]; It was the repository of Athens'' laws [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Basileios]; It was the site of the stone on which incoming magistrates swore their oath [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Basileios]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('908d7e5e-4c01-400d-b5d6-fe46c46afe6e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Dainzú is a Zapotec archaeological site located in the eastern side of the Valles Centrales de Oaxaca, about 20 km south-east of the city of Oaxaca, Oaxaca State.', 'Built of river cobbles of volcanic rock, the Zapotec site of Dainzú keeps a gallery of carved stone bas-reliefs, and nearby lies a large ball-court aligned east to west.', 'teaser-card-s024:908d7e5e-4c01-400d-b5d6-fe46c46afe6e:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 169 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8a911454b5981b3ab623a46ad03b0b8590333594b4031fc3f1ea1d8ea4cbec59|aec5846adaa37589fe07fe83f28e6df64457bb17791cda2b9971d829eca9d90d|aec5846adaa37589fe07fe83f28e6df64457bb17791cda2b9971d829eca9d90d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Built of river cobbles of volcanic rock, the Zapotec site of Dainzú keeps a gallery of carved stone bas-reliefs, and nearby lies a large ball-court aligned east to west."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site was built of river cobbles [S3]; the cobbles are of volcanic rock [S3]; Dainzu is a Zapotec site [S1]; it has a gallery of carved stone bas-reliefs [S4]; a large ball-court lies nearby [S5]; the ball-court is oriented east-west [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-051 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dainzú is a Zapotec archaeological site in Oaxaca, Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dainz%C3%BA]; Dainzú is an ancient village / settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dainz%C3%BA]; Dainzú is built of river cobbles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dainz%C3%BA]; The cobbles are of volcanic rock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dainz%C3%BA]; The site has a gallery of bas-reliefs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dainz%C3%BA]; The bas-reliefs are carved in stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dainz%C3%BA]; A large ball-court lies nearby [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dainz%C3%BA]; The ball-court is aligned east to west [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dainz%C3%BA]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('91889130-bd24-42d1-a128-89527ebf79f2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Inca aqueducts, canals, and waterfalls running through terraced rock. Whether it was a ritual bath, water temple, or engineering showcase remains uncertain.', 'Near Cusco, spring water still flows through three waterfalls over the terraced rocks of Tambomachay. Its function is uncertain: perhaps a military outpost, perhaps a spa for the Inca elite.', 'teaser-card-s024:91889130-bd24-42d1-a128-89527ebf79f2:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 190 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8ac0f90d0c11ab99ebe2fba9838de1e0f32c35532c083351333f29c5bb5bd235|9c07adeb7eabd6b0aa66ee5190fae1f16d9eadfce59b70e1f1cf5ae7fc8015c4|9c07adeb7eabd6b0aa66ee5190fae1f16d9eadfce59b70e1f1cf5ae7fc8015c4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Cusco, spring water still flows through three waterfalls over the terraced rocks of Tambomachay. Its function is uncertain: perhaps a military outpost, perhaps a spa for the Inca elite."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tambomachay is near Cusco [S1]; spring water flows through three waterfalls [S5]; the waterfalls still flow today [S5]; the water runs through terraced rocks [S3]; the site''s function is uncertain [S6]; it may have been a military outpost [S6]; it may have been a spa for the Inca elite [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-051 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tambomachay is an Inca archaeological site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]; It is near Cusco (Peru) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]; Spring water feeds the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]; There are three waterfalls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]; The waterfalls still flow today [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]; The waterfalls run over terraced rocks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]; Its function is uncertain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]; Possibly a military outpost [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]; Possibly a spa for the Inca elite [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambomachay]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9190f012-5a73-4f94-9f39-9c870a7bdf4c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A significant burial cairn, known as ''Skellaw Hill'', is found at the avenue''s northern end, though most stones have now been lost.', 'In the Eden Valley, Shap Stone Avenue ends at a burial cairn, Skellaw Hill. Its Goggleby Stone, cup-marked on its north face, led Tom Clare to suggest a Late Neolithic date.', 'teaser-card-s024:9190f012-5a73-4f94-9f39-9c870a7bdf4c:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 173 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '3ba25215cae4ed3c83f7abb331a46e4a5a837ebcc7d8c8804b501118d43c965e|7e5990ee6679b8bfe8ae77c8d821f33c80898c678437edc760085f53154b076f|7e5990ee6679b8bfe8ae77c8d821f33c80898c678437edc760085f53154b076f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Eden Valley, Shap Stone Avenue ends at a burial cairn, Skellaw Hill. Its Goggleby Stone, cup-marked on its north face, led Tom Clare to suggest a Late Neolithic date."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Shap Stone Avenue is in the Eden Valley [S1]; a burial cairn lies at the avenue''s (northern) end [S4]; the cairn is called Skellaw Hill [S4]; the Goggleby Stone belongs to the avenue [S5, S6]; the Goggleby Stone has a cup mark on its north face [S5]; Tom Clare suggested a Late Neolithic date for the avenue [S6]; his suggestion is based on evidence from the Goggleby Stone [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-051 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Shap Stone Avenue is a megalithic avenue of standing stones, near Shap, Cumbria, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; It lies in the Eden Valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; The avenue ends at a burial cairn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; The cairn is called Skellaw Hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; The Goggleby Stone belongs to the avenue [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; The Goggleby Stone is cup-marked [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; The cup mark is on the north face [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; Tom Clare suggested a date for the avenue [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; The date suggested is Late Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]; The suggestion rests on evidence of the Goggleby Stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shap_Stone_Avenue]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('927cd0d5-2d53-456d-b215-deb419ddf0b9'::uuid, 'card_description', '2nd–1st millennium BC. A conical hill forgotten for centuries — rediscovered in 1990 by Berkeley archaeologists. Plutarch records it as the site of a monument to two heroes of Chaeronea.', 'Its location gradually forgotten, Mount Thourion was found again in February 1990, with a marble block naming Homoloichos and Anaxidamos, heroes who showed Sulla''s troops a back way up.', 'teaser-card-s024:927cd0d5-2d53-456d-b215-deb419ddf0b9:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'b257046e0fbab0eba27cae39edfcbe26c842948817ab0ffbf5e9166e84bb6ef4|e3ab4b3906feb2b552811f63a6a31eb111666e30886500c0d216f8491dfcfe14|e3ab4b3906feb2b552811f63a6a31eb111666e30886500c0d216f8491dfcfe14', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Its location gradually forgotten, Mount Thourion was found again in February 1990, with a marble block naming Homoloichos and Anaxidamos, heroes who showed Sulla''s troops a back way up."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the location of Mount Thourion was gradually forgotten [S3]; it was rediscovered in February 1990 [S3]; a marble block was found with the rediscovery [S3, S7]; the block names Homoloichos and Anaxidamos [S7]; the two are called heroes [S7]; the two showed Sulla''s troops a back way up the hill [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-051 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Mount Thourion was a conical hill in Ancient Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]; Its location was gradually forgotten [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]; It was rediscovered [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]; The rediscovery was in February 1990 [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]; The find was a marble block [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]; The block names Homoloichos and Anaxidamos [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]; The block''s inscription calls them heroes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]; Homoloichos and Anaxidamos assisted Sulla''s troops [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]; They showed Sulla''s troops a back way up [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Thourion]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('934ed584-2c13-46fc-bf38-eadd53b68d6f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Cucuteni-Trypillia settlements, 5500–2750 BC, spanning southeastern Europe. Some housed thousands of people — among the largest Neolithic communities on Earth before vanishing entirely.', 'Settlements of the Cucuteni-Trypillia Culture: thousands of ruins from approximately 5500 to 2750 BC. Maidanetske may have held almost 3,000 houses, yet scholars do not call them cities.', 'teaser-card-s024:934ed584-2c13-46fc-bf38-eadd53b68d6f:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '43384883509e0774f3619e21288fb703f353a12d07b2ea0e7cad2d523033dda8|0954960ac65bf71ea0fb2aae2e8070ceb7a11cbbafae9f55799b6583c048a72c|0954960ac65bf71ea0fb2aae2e8070ceb7a11cbbafae9f55799b6583c048a72c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-018", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Settlements of the Cucuteni-Trypillia Culture: thousands of ruins from approximately 5500 to 2750 BC. Maidanetske may have held almost 3,000 houses, yet scholars do not call them cities."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-018 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the culture left behind thousands of settlement ruins [S2]; these date from approximately 5500 to 2750 BC [S2]; Maidanetske may have contained almost 3,000 houses (hedged) [S3]; scholars do not designate the giant settlements as cities [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-051 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Cucuteni-Trypillia culture left thousands of settlement ruins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Settlements_of_the_Cucuteni%E2%80%93Trypillia_culture]; The culture dates from approximately 5500 to 2750 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Settlements_of_the_Cucuteni%E2%80%93Trypillia_culture]; Maidanetske may have contained almost 3,000 houses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Settlements_of_the_Cucuteni%E2%80%93Trypillia_culture]; Scholars have not designated these settlements as cities [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Settlements_of_the_Cucuteni%E2%80%93Trypillia_culture]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('941ec7f7-645f-4c87-9cc5-f156e7504054'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Rock art near Qohaito appears to indicate habitation in the area since the fifth millennium BC, while the town is known to have survived to the sixth century AD.', 'Rock art near Qohaito appears to show people living here since the fifth millennium BC. Its stone ruins, once a stop on the trade route between Adulis and Aksum, were unexcavated as of 2011.', 'teaser-card-s024:941ec7f7-645f-4c87-9cc5-f156e7504054:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 190 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9f778909dac6cfc3a6062c78b9c419a3c8436aa4ccc5180bb86212fa7c14fb35|fb628c646da67419d39985e7719724768db992afb511b5576cf210d8b3c37030|fb628c646da67419d39985e7719724768db992afb511b5576cf210d8b3c37030', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-018", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Rock art near Qohaito appears to show people living here since the fifth millennium BC. Its stone ruins, once a stop on the trade route between Adulis and Aksum, were unexcavated as of 2011."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-018 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "rock art near Qohaito appears to indicate habitation (hedged) [S2]; that habitation dates back to the fifth millennium BC [S2]; Qohaito has stone ruins [S1]; the settlement was once a stop on the trade route between Adulis and Aksum [S4]; the stone ruins had not been excavated as of 2011 [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-052 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Qohaito is an ancient city/settlement with stone ruins in Eritrea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qohaito]; Rock art near Qohaito appears to show habitation in the area [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qohaito]; The habitation dates since the fifth millennium BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qohaito]; Qohaito was a stop on the trade route between Adulis and Aksum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qohaito]; The stone ruins were unexcavated as of 2011 [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qohaito]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('94c5cd62-041a-41c3-9e70-1b14573e0421'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The ninth fort on Hadrian''s Wall, built in the 2nd century AD to guard the Caw Gap. A rare Lorica Segmentata, a type of Roman plate armor, was found here.', 'Aesica, a Roman fort on Hadrian''s Wall, guarded the Caw Gap where the Haltwhistle Burn crosses the Wall, fed by a remarkable aqueduct that drew water directly into the fort.', 'teaser-card-s024:94c5cd62-041a-41c3-9e70-1b14573e0421:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 173 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5674432ab7aee1c6bf42ba8cc7c3796d05360961505576ff79d672806c0c9a4e|bf3c27959968b4b451ddb5ae197c166348a5cf737668003953ee53cef8f45be8|bf3c27959968b4b451ddb5ae197c166348a5cf737668003953ee53cef8f45be8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-018", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aesica, a Roman fort on Hadrian''s Wall, guarded the Caw Gap where the Haltwhistle Burn crosses the Wall, fed by a remarkable aqueduct that drew water directly into the fort."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-018 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aesica was a Roman fort [S1]; it lay on Hadrian''s Wall [S2]; it guarded the Caw Gap [S3]; at the Caw Gap the Haltwhistle Burn crosses the Wall [S3]; the fort was supplied by a remarkable aqueduct [S6]; the aqueduct drew water directly into the fort [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-052 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aesica was a Roman fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aesica]; The fort was on Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aesica]; Its main purpose was to guard the Caw Gap [SUPPORTED: ht