    ('94e18246-8ec7-4da8-9bad-ea33e78d2dcb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman crossroads city where the Crna meets the Vardar. Stobi was capital of Macedonia Salutaris and a strategic center for trade and military campaigns.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s024:94e18246-8ec7-4da8-9bad-ea33e78d2dcb:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (card-clear-unproven-after-verify): card cleared (unproven-after-verify)', '||376efd97646d83b2362e6c7c9eff68002bd4057d543cb4e67f6198ea3e711c20', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: unproven-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: Stobi is an ancient town/settlement in North Macedonia, capital of a Roman province; The web check could not prove: Stobi was an ancient town (North Macedonia)"}]'::jsonb),
    ('952e9faf-49f9-4fe9-8cee-f0934167b9df'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Capital of the Aksumite Empire from the 4th century BC, Axum commanded Red Sea trade and raised obelisks reaching 33 metres — the tallest monolithic stone monuments ever erected.', 'Wheat is depicted on Aksumite coins, hinting that Axum likely had a prosperous agricultural vicinity, with abundant livestock and industries such as metalworking, glassware and pottery.', 'teaser-card-s024:952e9faf-49f9-4fe9-8cee-f0934167b9df:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'ae7f760e85f036d47cd38127cda007ef8a5b2e56e49d702e9ef4ad13225cda7f|f82719b06b0227627dca1de460cc95cf805e2c9a0f8a616c3e4b9ce5bdfa6af0|f82719b06b0227627dca1de460cc95cf805e2c9a0f8a616c3e4b9ce5bdfa6af0', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wheat is depicted on Aksumite coins, hinting that Axum likely had a prosperous agricultural vicinity, with abundant livestock and industries such as metalworking, glassware and pottery."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "wheat is depicted on Aksumite coins [S1]; Axum likely had a prosperous agricultural vicinity (hedged) [S1]; abundant livestock [S1]; industries such as metalworking, glassware and pottery [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-005 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Axum is a town in Ethiopia and the site of the historic capital of the Aksumite Empire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axum]; Wheat is depicted on Aksumite coins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axum]; Axum likely had a prosperous agricultural vicinity [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axum]; Axum had abundant livestock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axum]; Axum had industries such as metalworking, glassware and pottery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9556fc32-13d4-4a64-b14e-bfd85af6b4c5'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A small hillfort enclosing just 0.2 hectares — one of three forts on the same hillside. Its rubble bank forms an oval within dense woodland.', 'Overlooking Baglan from Briton Ferry Woods, the small hillfort of Craig Ty-Isaf is ringed by rubble banks some 2.5m high and 9m wide, cut by a gap that formed its original entrance.', 'teaser-card-s024:9556fc32-13d4-4a64-b14e-bfd85af6b4c5:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8006d69b6ff22c62ce87312f74269dc2bc4ae776b0cee8abcbca2e9f7f64e57a|719ed6c3b910630ef9bd0b9fb0beb44ee1e9824af9aec60596cb11817e22c80d|719ed6c3b910630ef9bd0b9fb0beb44ee1e9824af9aec60596cb11817e22c80d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-018", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Overlooking Baglan from Briton Ferry Woods, the small hillfort of Craig Ty-Isaf is ringed by rubble banks some 2.5m high and 9m wide, cut by a gap that formed its original entrance."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-018 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Craig Ty-Isaf is a small hillfort [S1]; it overlooks Baglan [S3]; it is within Briton Ferry Woods [S2]; it is ringed by rubble banks [S4, S6]; the rubble banks are some 2.5m high and 9m wide [S6]; a gap through the banks formed the original entrance [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-052 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Craig Ty-Isaf is a small hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Craig_Ty-Isaf]; It lies within Briton Ferry Woods [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Craig_Ty-Isaf]; It overlooks Baglan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Craig_Ty-Isaf]; Rubble banks ring the hillfort (an oval of rubble banks) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Craig_Ty-Isaf]; The rubble banks are some 2.5m high and 9m wide [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Craig_Ty-Isaf]; A gap through the banks formed the original entrance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Craig_Ty-Isaf]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('95817f28-f648-440a-b29f-f5ac3f794a69'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A dividing wall between the northern and southern halves of the fort allowed the north part to store supplies from sea-going ships, whilst the southern part remained a garrison.', 'Above the mouth of the River Tyne, Arbeia was built in 129 AD. Its northern half stored supplies from sea-going ships, and its final garrison were once barge-men from the River Tigris.', 'teaser-card-s024:95817f28-f648-440a-b29f-f5ac3f794a69:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '3956a5d7f1bbf14bc566827a07883fa68396a68881313d41f51fff99d4a15a75|f30dda2e65a4c4ddd52a1a26575460630ca3832536112e619b534ad8670a9cbf|f30dda2e65a4c4ddd52a1a26575460630ca3832536112e619b534ad8670a9cbf', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-018", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Above the mouth of the River Tyne, Arbeia was built in 129 AD. Its northern half stored supplies from sea-going ships, and its final garrison were once barge-men from the River Tigris."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-018 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Arbeia stands above (overlooking) the mouth of the River Tyne [S3]; the fort was built in 129 AD [S3]; its northern half stored supplies from sea-going ships [S6]; its final garrison were originally barge-men from the River Tigris [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-053 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Arbeia is a Roman fort (at South Shields, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbeia]; Arbeia lies above/overlooking the mouth of the River Tyne [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbeia]; The fort was built in 129 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbeia]; Its northern half stored supplies from sea-going ships [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbeia]; Its final garrison were barge-men from the River Tigris [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbeia]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('95c0ba9a-11f1-4d7d-9cbc-c099c0a944cf'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Pyramid G1-c was originally not a part of Khufu''s pyramid complex, as its southern side is aligned not with the side of the Great Pyramid, but with Khufukhaf I''s mastaba tomb nearby.', 'Pyramid G1-c aligns its southern side not with the Great Pyramid but with the nearby mastaba of Khufukhaf I: an unfinished pyramid, built in a hurry and without a boat pit.', 'teaser-card-s024:95c0ba9a-11f1-4d7d-9cbc-c099c0a944cf:card_description', 'teaser-card-s024 (teaser-card): teaser card, 172 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'b63539367e3bb5bec93939a2e7de1c407003cf33158aa1155442f11975a44dcd|1a03130294819feaa336af85a7c8309a1602460753c7a600410bb0a919199bfe|1a03130294819feaa336af85a7c8309a1602460753c7a600410bb0a919199bfe', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-018", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pyramid G1-c aligns its southern side not with the Great Pyramid but with the nearby mastaba of Khufukhaf I: an unfinished pyramid, built in a hurry and without a boat pit."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-018 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pyramid G1-c''s southern side is not aligned with the Great Pyramid [S1]; its southern side is aligned with the nearby mastaba of Khufukhaf I [S1]; it is an unfinished pyramid [S3]; it was built in a hurry [S3]; it has no boat pit [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-053 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pyramid G1-c is a subsidiary pyramid at Giza, Egypt [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyramid_G1-c]; Its southern side is not aligned with the Great Pyramid [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P